#
# SOR: Rocco's nu(G), Sidki's chi(G) and related groups
#
# chi(G, S; m): weak commutativity imposed only on products of generators
# (declarations).
#
#! @Chapter The weak commutativity group
#!
#! @Section Reduced presentations: weak commutativity on products of generators
#!
#! Let $S$ be a generating set of $G$ and $m \geq 1$. Oliveira and Sidki
#! (Bull. Braz. Math. Soc. 40, 2009, Section 4) define
#! $$\chi(G, S; m) = \langle G, G^\psi \mid [a, a^\psi] = 1
#!   \ \forall a \in S \cup S^2 \cup \cdots \cup S^m \rangle,$$
#! where $S^i$ is the set of products of $i$ elements of $S$. There are two
#! copies of $G$, as in $\chi(G)$, and $m$ bounds the length of the products;
#! this is not the group $\chi_n(H)$ with $n$ copies of
#! <Ref Oper="SidkiChiMotherGroup" Label="for IsGroup, IsPosInt"/>. The group
#! $\chi(G)$ is a quotient of $\chi(G, S; m)$, and equal to it when $m$ is large.
#!
#! The group $\chi(G, S; m)$ may be infinite for finite $G$: for
#! $G = C_2 \times C_2$ and $S = \{a_1, a_2\}$, $\chi(G, S; 1)$ contains a free
#! abelian group of rank $2$; and for $G = C_2^3$ the kernel of
#! $\chi(G, S; 2) \to \chi(G)$ is free abelian (Theorem 8). On the other hand,
#! $\chi(G, S; 2) = \chi(G)$ when $G$ is abelian and generated by two elements
#! (Corollary 1), and when $G$ is finite abelian of odd order (Corollary 2).
#! So the package returns the finitely presented group, with the embeddings of
#! the two copies and the projection onto $\chi(G)$, also finitely presented;
#! quotients can be computed from it, for instance by <C>EpimorphismPGroup</C>
#! with a bound on the class.

#! @Description
#!   Returns the finitely presented group $\chi(G, S; m)$, where <A>S</A> is a
#!   generating set of the finite group <A>G</A> (by default
#!   <C>GeneratorsOfGroup(</C><A>G</A><C>)</C>) and <A>m</A> a positive integer.
#!   <C>Embedding(</C><A>C</A><C>, 1)</C> and <C>Embedding(</C><A>C</A><C>, 2)</C>
#!   are the embeddings of $G$ and $G^\psi$, and
#!   <Ref Attr="SidkiChiHatProjection" Label="for IsGroup"/> is the projection
#!   onto $\chi(G)$.
#! @Arguments G, m[, S]
#! @Returns a finitely presented group
DeclareGlobalFunction( "SidkiChiHatGroup" );

#! @Description
#!   For <A>C</A> $= \chi(G, S; m)$ returned by
#!   <Ref Func="SidkiChiHatGroup"/>, the natural epimorphism onto $\chi(G)$
#!   as a finitely presented group
#!   (<Ref Oper="SidkiChiGroup" Label="for IsGroup, IsString"/> with
#!   <C>"fp"</C>), which maps each copy of $G$ to the corresponding copy:
#!   $\chi(G)$ has the same generators and more relations, so the projection
#!   exists for every finite $G$. Its kernel is trivial exactly when
#!   $\chi(G, S; m) = \chi(G)$. Composed with
#!   <C>Embedding(</C><A>C</A><C>, </C><A>i</A><C>)</C> it gives the embedding
#!   of the $i$-th copy of $G$ into $\chi(G)$; since that one is injective, so
#!   are the embeddings of $G$ into $\chi(G, S; m)$, also when this group is
#!   infinite. In the finitely presented $\chi(G)$, <C>Kernel</C> and
#!   <C>Size</C> go through coset enumeration; when $G$ is in one of the
#!   classes of <Q>Strategy by class of groups</Q>, the projection with
#!   <C>"auto"</C> below is much faster.
#! @Arguments C
#! @Returns a homomorphism
DeclareAttribute( "SidkiChiHatProjection", IsGroup );

#! @Description
#!   The same projection, onto $\chi(G)$ computed by
#!   <C>SidkiChiGroup(</C><A>G</A><C>, </C><A>method</A><C>)</C>; for instance
#!   <C>"auto"</C> gives a group in which one can compute (a pc group, a pcp
#!   group or a subgroup of a direct product), when $G$ is in one of the
#!   classes of <Q>Strategy by class of groups</Q>.
#! @Arguments C, method
#! @Returns a homomorphism
DeclareOperation( "SidkiChiHatProjection", [ IsGroup, IsString ] );

#! @BeginExampleSession
#! gap> SetInfoLevel( InfoSOR, 0 );
#! gap> A := ElementaryAbelianGroup( 4 );;
#! gap> C := SidkiChiHatGroup( A, 1 );;
#! gap> K := Kernel( SidkiChiHatProjection( C ) );;   # finite index in C
#! gap> AbelianInvariants( K );    # infinite: Z x Z
#! [ 0, 0 ]
#! gap> IsPcGroup( Range( SidkiChiHatProjection( C, "auto" ) ) );
#! true
#! gap> Size( SidkiChiHatGroup( A, 2 ) ) = Size( SidkiChiGroup( A ) );   # Corollary 1
#! true
#! gap> A := ElementaryAbelianGroup( 27 );;   # odd order: Corollary 2
#! gap> P := Image( EpimorphismPGroup( SidkiChiHatGroup( A, 2 ), 3 ) );;
#! gap> Size( P ) = Size( SidkiChiGroup( A ) );
#! true
#! @EndExampleSession

# Internal data of chi(G, S; m).
DeclareAttribute( "RS_ChiHatData", IsGroup );
